WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.840
There were so many bloopers and gags produced&nbsp;
in The Office that they could almost make up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:07.200
their own show. Some of those cut gags&nbsp;
were even funnier than the final version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:10.560
of the scene. Here is the best of those&nbsp;
character-breaking, line-fumbling moments.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:14.320
Now that he's broken up with Jan, Michael&nbsp;
is pitifully lonely and desperate enough to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:17.440
become borderline obsessed with the&nbsp;
model in an office supply catalog.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:20.400
Desperate for companionship before&nbsp;
he gets too old and can't have kids,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:23.840
Michael demands his subordinates each write down&nbsp;
the name of a single woman they know that he could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:28.160
go on a date with. In the original scene, after a&nbsp;
whole afternoon of Michael's self-pitying antics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:32.160
Pam relents and says she has someone for&nbsp;
him: her poor, unsuspecting landlady.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:32.660
"Michael?"

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:34.500
"Uh."

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:36.800
"Are you Michael Scott?"

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:39.920
The blooper during this scene came in&nbsp;
the form of a poorly delivered line.

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:44.834
"Is — Is she hot? That was such a bad take!"

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:49.600
Ryan Howard, introduced to us in the&nbsp;
pilot episode as a mild-mannered temp,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:53.120
has risen to the rank of corporate VP&nbsp;
by the beginning of season four. When he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:57.200
reintroduces himself to "his favorite branch" in&nbsp;
Scranton, neither his five o'clock shadow nor his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:01.440
Bluetooth nor his new, expensive suit succeed in&nbsp;
getting anyone to respect him the way he wants.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:05.120
The people who don't like him continue not&nbsp;
liking him, and the people who do won't stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:09.120
treating him like he's still the adorable little&nbsp;
office temp that everyone likes to pick on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:12.720
In the original scene, Ryan snaps and&nbsp;
admonishes Michael in front of the office.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:16.080
"I am your boss now. You're gonna have to&nbsp;
treat me the same way you treated Jan."

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:19.840
In the blooper, Steve Carrell and Brian&nbsp;
Baumgartner imitated him, and he began laughing.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:21.880
"But you--"

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.520
After he finished laughing, BJ apologized.

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:30.240
"I'm really sorry I broke, that was too good."

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:34.400
This episode begins with a despondent Phyllis&nbsp;
telling her coworkers that she was flashed by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:38.160
a man in a trench coat after she stopped to&nbsp;
help him with directions in the parking lot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:40.240
Michael goes back and forth between making crass,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:43.840
inappropriate jokes and trying to host a&nbsp;
series of women's appreciation seminars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:48.000
and events for which, as Jim points out,&nbsp;
he is completely unqualified to manage.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:54.080
"I'd like to apologise for all of the men&nbsp;
who thought this was a laughing matter."

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:58.240
Meanwhile, Dwight announces the creation&nbsp;
of a very serious indeed anti-flashing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:02.000
task force that will be run entirely by&nbsp;
him, even though he has no clue how to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:05.920
investigate a crime and local police are&nbsp;
already on the job. While explaining the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:10.240
task force to his coworkers, he refers to&nbsp;
Phyllis as "phallus" before apologizing.

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:13.120
"Phyllis, sorry. I've got penises on the brain."

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:15.200
"Back to work everybody."

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:18.480
Naturally, a scene like this would be&nbsp;
hard to get through with a straight face.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:22.000
The cast tried it multiple times,&nbsp;
breaking constantly. At one point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:25.200
Rainn Wilson, who played Dwight, tried&nbsp;
to muscle through and complete the scene.

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:28.560
"That is a risk that we have to— No,&nbsp;
no, no, I know what you're saying."

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:29.440
"Why do you— Why do you keep going?"

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:35.280
With Michael and Pam out giving sales training&nbsp;
presentations to the other regional branches,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:37.200
Jim and Dwight are in charge of the office.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:41.200
Things immediately take a turn for the worse when&nbsp;
they realize they've forgotten Kelly's birthday.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:44.560
Kelly is angry, and when Dwight stands&nbsp;
up for Jim, she grows even angrier.

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:48.296
"Yeah, screw you, beet farmer! I don't&nbsp;
forget your birthday. I would never do that."

00:02:48.296 --> 00:02:49.280
"Guys, Guys, Guys."

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:52.480
Jim tries to salvage the situation&nbsp;
before Dwight yells back and makes it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:55.360
worse by promising her a cake&nbsp;
and a party that afternoon.

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:58.640
It's probably not that surprising that a scene&nbsp;
like this might've been a little too much fun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:02.560
to shoot. In other takes, every time John&nbsp;
Krasinski steps in to lower the temperature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:05.040
Rainn Wilson shoots him a shocked, wide-eyed look.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:07.120
"Hey! Guys. C—"

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:10.400
"Stop doing that. It makes no sense."

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:14.160
By the third failure, they're all a little bit&nbsp;
frustrated, but still having plenty of fun.

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:16.640
At the beginning of the fifth season, the Scranton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:20.160
branch participates in a company-wide&nbsp;
weight loss competition. Naturally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:24.800
things go astray. Kelly starves herself and&nbsp;
tries to buy a tapeworm from Creed. Dwight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:28.320
after insulting several of his coworkers for&nbsp;
their weight, tricks Phyllis into going on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:33.120
non-existent sales call, only to abandon her,&nbsp;
forcing her to burn calories on her walk back.

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:34.640
"There was no client.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:38.800
Dwight drove me to an abandoned warehouse&nbsp;
five miles away and pushed me out of the car.”

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:42.800
Holly sets up an HR meeting to address the&nbsp;
madness, but Michael ruins it by donning a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:46.000
semi-deflated sumo suit and appearing&nbsp;
in character as "Michael Klump,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:48.400
an insulting "celebration" of overweight people.

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:52.480
"This big, fat pig is beautiful."

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:56.960
The original scene is an Office classic,&nbsp;
but the gag version is even better.

00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:01.003
"Well, hello everybodyyyy."

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:09.040
Carrell had to try multiple takes before&nbsp;
everyone was able to hold in their laughter.

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:13.440
An excited Andy and a not-so-excited&nbsp;
Angela have decided, at her insistence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:17.520
to book Schrute Farms as their wedding venue, and&nbsp;
are planning a lunch break trip to check it out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:20.960
Problem is, proprietor Dwight doesn't&nbsp;
know how to give acceptable directions.

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:23.360
"Walk until you hear the beehive."

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:26.800
When Andy brings this up, Dwight insists&nbsp;
the directions are perfectly normal.

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:27.840
"How could it be more clear?"

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:31.440
In the bloopers from this scene, nobody&nbsp;
can keep a straight face as Ed Helms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:34.160
tries out some bizarre, obviously ad-libbed lines.

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:37.920
"If you are attacked by territorial crows—"

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:41.200
It clearly took quite a few&nbsp;
tries to get a usable take.

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:45.440
Pam is pregnant with her and Jim's first child,&nbsp;
but she's resisting everyone's attempts to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:49.440
her to the hospital. If you ask her, she'd say she&nbsp;
wants to wait until after midnight for insurance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:53.920
purposes, but the reality is that she's scared&nbsp;
of going into labor and wants to drag it out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:57.280
Jim is slowly losing it trying to get her&nbsp;
to the hospital, and at one point relies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:00.880
on Michael to tell him when her contractions&nbsp;
start coming every five minutes. When he finds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:04.880
out they're now coming every two minutes, he&nbsp;
yells at Michael for not informing him earlier.

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:08.720
"We waited too long. Two minutes doesn't&nbsp;
do us any good. Well, what happened to four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:09.520
and three minutes?!"

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:12.880
In other takes, the actors struggled to&nbsp;
make it through this scene without cracking.

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:13.440
"No, what—"

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:15.524
"You gotta hold it together, okay?"

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:21.280
The kickoff to season 4 is Meredith ending&nbsp;
up hospitalized after Michael hits her with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:25.200
his car in the parking lot. She has a minor&nbsp;
pelvic fracture and gets a rabies shot after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:28.560
the doctor finds out she might've been bitten&nbsp;
by a bat when one was trapped in the office&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:32.000
during the previous season. This prompts&nbsp;
a guilt-stricken Michael to launch a "Fun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:36.400
Run" race for the rabies cure, despite the fact&nbsp;
that Meredith likely doesn't have rabies — and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:40.160
as Jim pointed out, it's already been&nbsp;
cured. While he's organizing all that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:44.160
Pam rallies the coworkers for a lunch break&nbsp;
carpool to visit Meredith in the hospital.

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:47.760
In a scene that didn't make the final cut,&nbsp;
Steve Carrell can't get his line right.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:51.760
When Jenna Fischer tells everyone, quote, "Okay,&nbsp;
we're leaving for the hospital at one," Carrell is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:56.400
supposed to respond with, quote, "like a freedom&nbsp;
tree?" Instead, he trips all over the line.

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:58.960
"Okay, so, we're all leaving&nbsp;
for the hospital at 1 o'clock."

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:00.720
"Yes, so fab, fruh-freedom tree."

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:01.360
"I can take three—"

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:08.640
In season 7, Jim, Dwight, and even Michael,&nbsp;
all seasoned and talented salesmen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:11.760
lose a major potential client to&nbsp;
rival traveling rep Danny Cordray,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:15.520
of Osprey Paper. Michael promptly calls&nbsp;
the sales team to a brainstorming session&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:19.120
upon returning to the office, and asks&nbsp;
everyone for ideas to outmaneuver Cordray.

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:21.760
"We sell better."

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:26.240
Michael mistakes Stanley's sarcasm&nbsp;
for indifference, tells him to leave,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:30.880
and suggests he has diabetes. Stanley insists&nbsp;
he doesn't have diabetes and would like to stay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:34.400
for once, as Cordray affected his&nbsp;
paycheck, but he eventually leaves.

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:39.280
"See? I could tell by the sound you made when&nbsp;
you stood up that you have— Okay, yes Phillis?"

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:41.760
That scene took several tries to nail. Initially,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:45.680
when Michael said his last line to Stanley,&nbsp;
every cast member in the room lost it.

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:48.800
"See, just the noise you made&nbsp;
when you stood up, I could—”

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:53.600
One of the major storylines of season three&nbsp;
was the merging of the defunct Stamford,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:57.440
Connecticut branch with the one in Scranton.&nbsp;
Martin, one of the Stamford transplants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:01.520
is a former convict who'd been briefly imprisoned&nbsp;
for insider trading, long before starting his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:05.360
career with Dunder Mifflin. Once he finds out&nbsp;
about this, Michael tries awkwardly defending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:08.720
Martin, simply because he's Black and&nbsp;
Michael is worried about stereotypes.

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:15.120
"But I just want to declare publicly&nbsp;
that I trust him completely."

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:18.800
Then, when his subordinates start inquiring about&nbsp;
prison and pretending to wish they were in it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:21.760
Michael sees Martin as a threat&nbsp;
to office morale and his "fun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:24.720
boss" shtick. In one scene,&nbsp;
Michael barges into the break room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:28.160
where Martin is casually explaining&nbsp;
his prison time to his new coworkers.

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:32.640
"Been a lot of fun talk about prison&nbsp;
today, but I'm here to scare you straight."

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:35.280
In the bloopers, not knowing the&nbsp;
camera was rolling, a crew member&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:39.440
audibly starts talking in the background,&nbsp;
forcing everyone to reset for a new take.

00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:41.040
"We're rolling, Alyssa."
"Right, how are ya?"

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:42.320
"I thought somebody just said 'Mel.'"

00:07:42.320 --> 00:07:44.320
In another take, Steve Carrell is delivering lines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:46.880
in his iconic purple bandana&nbsp;
when the group begins laughing.

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:48.320
"Where did you pinch him?"

00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:54.800
Dunder Mifflin Infinity, Ryan Howard's new&nbsp;
paper-buying online portal that he hopes will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.800 --> 00:07:59.040
modernize the ailing company, is going live.&nbsp;
To celebrate, the office decides to order some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:03.600
pizza for a branch party. Michael comes out of his&nbsp;
office to announce that hot food is on the way.

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:04.560
"Good news."

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:05.680
"We get to go home?"

00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:09.600
In a different take, Leslie David Baker&nbsp;
doesn't just say that line — he shouts it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:13.600
joyously, wide-eyed, throwing his hands&nbsp;
into the air and leaning back in his seat.

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:15.520
"I have some good news."

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:16.800
"We get to go home!"

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:21.040
This prompts Steve Carrell, followed quickly by&nbsp;
the entire cast, to break into uncontrollable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:26.320
laughter. Leslie himself is laughing harder than&nbsp;
anyone. John Krasinski is howling so hard he holds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.320 --> 00:08:31.200
his hands over his face. If you don't at least&nbsp;
crack up watching this, you may be dead inside.

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:35.280
If you're watching a video about The Office,&nbsp;
you've almost certainly seen what's arguably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:39.760
its greatest episode: Season 4's Dinner Party.&nbsp;
Michael and his borderline psychotic girlfriend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:43.920
and former boss, Jan, host an after-work&nbsp;
get-together at their condo with Jim, Pam,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:48.000
Andy, Angela, Dwight, and Dwight's former&nbsp;
babysitter, who he brought as a date.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:51.280
The episode, in which Michael and Jan's&nbsp;
relationship unravels before the eyes of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:54.720
their squirming guests over the course of&nbsp;
an evening filled with awkward house tours,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:58.720
failed business pitches, games that nobody&nbsp;
likes, a screaming match about having kids,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:03.280
passive-aggressive arguments between the hosts,&nbsp;
a very late meal that both Pam and Michael think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:08.160
Jan has poisoned, and much more, is a triumph&nbsp;
of cringe comedy that's never been matched.

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:12.080
"Michael and Jan seem to be&nbsp;
playing their own separate game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:15.360
and it's called, 'Let's see how&nbsp;
uncomfortable we can make our guests.'"

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:19.600
The blooper reel is even more entertaining.&nbsp;
The episode was famously hard to shoot because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:23.360
everyone in it simply could not stop&nbsp;
laughing long enough to get good takes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:27.840
In one take, Steve Carrell talks about Michael's&nbsp;
tiny plasma screen TV before saying, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:30.160
"Right over here, Jim" to John Krasinski.

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:36.160
"I guess what surround sounds means&nbsp;
is that— Right over here, Jim."

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:37.840
Co-writer Lee Eisenberg said,

00:09:37.840 --> 00:09:42.160
"There's nothing more satisfying than having Steve&nbsp;
Carell barely able to get through his lines."

00:09:42.160 --> 00:09:46.800
"Oh, Jesus, this is terrible.&nbsp;
We'll never finish this episode."

